  {
    "episode_id": 92201,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92201.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that's a very good question. I think anybody gets elected. It feels not only elated, but it feels very grateful because, oftentimes, your polls don't show you winning. And my poll showed me that I was losing. In fact, two weeks out is over 10% or 15%. And I finally won by about 3%. And as you say, Jordan, it was a 66% Democrat district. It was set up so that the former speaker of the Florida House, John Mills, could take the seat easily. He had no primary opposition. They were four of us, all the other five of us in the primary. And we went through a primary. And at that point, Jordan, I lost. I came in second in the initial race. And in Florida, the rules were you had to have a runoff. And then I won the runoff. So it was an arduous campaign. And I was grateful to win. And I can only say that I think in the end that I had much higher profile because I think the speaker of the house felt that he was a shoe in and that he did not get out and I was out there constantly. So I would say partly it's because I worked harder and then obviously I think that George H.W. Bush winning presidency has some coattails effect too.",
