    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, that was part of it. The problem was, at that point, is that small states felt they'd be dominated by the big states. So you had the small states, like Delaware and New Hampshire, and then you had New York and Virginia and Pennsylvania with large states. When you went to the Senate, how do you proportion the power? Well, part of the compromise came from, delegate from Connecticut was Sheridan, was it basically will allow the small states to have two U.S. senators and the large states to the U.S. senators. So New York and New Hampshire would have the same power in the Senate. That was part of the compromise. The other part of the compromise, as you mentioned, is that the Southern states felt that they had a huge population of African Americans, and they wanted them counted as citizens so that they could have more representatives in the House of Representatives. And the compromise was to allow, unfortunately, as highly racial was to say African-Americans were three-fifths of America. So",
