    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Well, the European Union is a huge bureaucracy that's over all these 28 countries, these sovereign countries. A lot of very strong prestigious leaders argued for that coalition of the European Union so we could prevent another world war. But what has happened is the layer of the European Union bureaucracy on a sovereign country, for example Spain or Greece or England or France or Germany, creates another whole set of legislative issues and I think that to have the United European Union to be equal to the United States of America is sometimes not working because the United States is a very young country. But when you go to some of these countries in Europe, there have been around for four or five because Charlemagne goes back to the Roman times, back to 300 AD. So the problem is that there's too much ingrained history in each sovereign country So breaking it up, making it all together into one strong United European of Europe, I think it's not going to work as well as the United States because our state's individual states are a lot different than the individual 28 countries. So the analogy is not quite perfect for the success of the European Union with the United States of America.",
